CAT = "sgsnbMental"; SEG = "MHA"
Q = [
("A patient says he has been thinking about ending his life. What is the nurse's best response?",
 'Ask directly about plans; stay with him', ['Change the subject to something positive', 'Promise to keep what he says secret', 'Tell him he has so much to live for'],
 'Asking directly about suicidal thoughts does not plant the idea. The nurse assesses risk, stays with the patient, and escalates for urgent assessment, never promising secrecy.'),
('Which finding is most typical of delirium?',
 'Sudden confusion that fluctuates', ['Gradual memory loss over years', 'Stable low mood for months', 'Persistent fixed beliefs for years'],
 'Delirium has an acute onset, fluctuating attention and awareness, and is usually due to an underlying illness, infection or medicine.'),
('Which feature best separates dementia from delirium?',
 'Gradual onset with a progressive course', ['A sudden onset over a few hours', 'It is always completely reversible', 'Alertness changes from hour to hour'],
 'Dementia develops over months to years with progressive decline, while delirium is sudden and fluctuating.'),
('A patient with dementia becomes agitated in the evening. Which approach helps most?',
 'A calm routine in a quiet familiar space', ['Arguing that his belief is wrong', 'Restricting all visitors completely', 'Giving sedatives straight away'],
 'Sundowning is eased by routine, light, low noise and checking for pain, hunger or toilet needs; sedatives are a last resort.'),
('A patient with schizophrenia says the nurse is poisoning his food. What is the best approach?',
 'Acknowledge his fear without arguing', ['Agree that the food has been poisoned', 'Tell him sharply that he is wrong', 'Ignore the statement and walk away'],
 'The nurse accepts his feelings, states reality calmly and does not support or argue with the delusion.'),
('Which is a common side effect of antipsychotic drugs that the nurse should watch for?',
 'Muscle stiffness and tremor', ['Excessive hunger for sugar only', 'Raised pain tolerance', 'Rapid fluid loss'],
 'Extrapyramidal effects such as rigidity, tremor and restlessness occur, and metabolic changes are monitored.'),
('A patient taking lithium should be monitored for which problem?',
 'Toxicity from a high blood level', ['Low calcium levels only', 'Vitamin K deficiency and bleeding', 'Hearing loss in both ears'],
 'Lithium has a narrow therapeutic range, so blood levels, renal function and signs of toxicity such as tremor, diarrhoea and confusion are monitored.'),
('Which approach is appropriate for a patient who is verbally aggressive?',
 'Stay calm, keep distance, speak softly', ['Raise your voice to establish control', 'Block the exit with your body', 'Touch his arm firmly to calm him'],
 'De-escalation uses calm tone, respect for personal space and an unobstructed exit for both, with help called if the risk rises.'),
("What is the nurse's responsibility when a patient is admitted to a mental health ward involuntarily?",
 'Respect his rights and give clear information', ['Withhold information for his own good', 'Give all treatment without explanation', 'Limit visitors without reasons'],
 'People detained under mental health law still keep dignity, information and the right to be involved in decisions as far as possible.'),
('Which of these is a sign of depression in an older adult?',
 'Withdrawal, poor appetite, poor sleep', ['Increased energy and very little sleep', 'Excessive friendliness with everyone', 'Sudden sharpening of his memory'],
 'Depression in older adults often appears as withdrawal, reduced appetite, sleep changes and physical complaints, and it can be mistaken for dementia.'),
('Which technique helps reduce the risk of delirium in hospital?',
 'Orientation cues, sleep, early mobility', ['Bed rest with the lights on all night', 'Routine use of physical restraints', 'Limiting glasses and hearing aids'],
 'Reorientation, glasses and hearing aids, sleep hygiene, hydration and mobilisation reduce delirium.'),
("Which assessment is most important before the first dose of an older adult's new sedative?",
 'Falls risk and current medicines', ['His favourite foods and drinks', "His family's occupations", 'His shoe size and clothing'],
 'Older adults are more sensitive to sedatives, so the risk of falls, confusion and interactions with other medicines is assessed.'),
('A frail older adult has lost 5 kg in two months. Which action is most appropriate?',
 'Assess nutrition; refer to a dietitian', ['Assume it is normal ageing', 'Reduce his meal portions further', 'Wait another six months before acting'],
 'Unintentional weight loss needs a nutritional assessment, review of causes such as dentition, depression or illness, and dietitian input.'),
("Which care practice supports an older adult's dignity during personal care?",
 'Offer choices and keep him covered', ['Speak to the carer instead of him', "Use terms like 'dear' for everyone", 'Hurry him to save time'],
 'Dignity is maintained by privacy, choice, respectful language and addressing the person directly.'),
('A patient nearing the end of life says he is afraid to die. What is a helpful response?',
 'Listen and acknowledge his feelings', ['Tell him not to think about it', 'Say he will be fine soon', 'Leave the conversation to the doctor'],
 'Active listening and presence support emotional and spiritual needs. Offering false reassurance blocks communication.'),
('What is the aim of palliative care?',
 'Quality of life and symptom relief', ['Curing the underlying illness at all costs', "Shortening the patient's life", 'Prolonging life whatever the burden'],
 'Palliative care aims to relieve pain and other distressing symptoms and to support the patient and family, from diagnosis onwards if needed.'),
('Which is true about breathlessness at the end of life?',
 'A fan, upright position and opioids may help', ['It cannot be treated at all', 'It always means oxygen is needed', 'It should be ignored if he is calm'],
 'Non-drug measures such as a fan, upright position and calm presence are used with prescribed low-dose opioids for symptom relief.'),
('A dying patient has noisy, rattling respirations. How should the nurse support the family?',
 'Explain it is usual and not distressing to him', ['Tell them he is choking', 'Suction him repeatedly to clear it', 'Ask the family to leave the room'],
 'Pooled secretions in the last hours cause a rattle that is usually not distressing to the patient; explaining this reduces family distress.'),
('Which action is part of care after death?',
 'Treat the body with respect', ['Leave it unattended until morning', 'Discuss the cause of death with visitors', 'Delay informing the family for a day'],
 "Care after death follows policy and the family's cultural and religious needs, with dignity and privacy preserved."),
("What does a person's advance care plan record?",
 'His wishes for future care', ['Only his current list of medicines', 'His insurance policy details', 'Only his funeral costs and plans'],
 "An advance care plan records a person's values and preferences for future treatment so that they can guide care if he cannot decide."),
]
